WEBVTT

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:27.360
Antarctica, the continent of the penguins.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:29.800
Busy little Adelis.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:38.020
Stately emperors and their close relatives, the king penguins.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.480
Summer is already giving way to the long winter night.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.100
First storms of autumn, most penguins head for the warmer north.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:17.130
The emperors alone brace for winter, in 90 mile an hour winds and temperatures of 50 below, they lay their eggs.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.790
How do these birds cope with such extreme weather?

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:38.000
And as the climate starts to change, what lies ahead for the penguins of the Antarctic?

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:46.600
Ice. Thousands and thousands of miles of ice, on both the sea and the land.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:54.150
Antarctica's mainland is larger than Europe, but it lies under an ice cap two miles thick.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.970
In late autumn, life retreats to a few places where the first

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.340
The frozen land meets the frozen sea.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.720
This dot on the northwest coast is one of them,

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.500
an emperor penguin colony.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.480
The world's most grueling winter is on its way,

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.860
but the emperors are starting new families.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.820
They've courted and mated, and the eggs are laid, one per pair.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.560
The mother won't brood the egg, the father will.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.100
And one of the most precarious parts of the whole process is the handover.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.590
Too many seconds on the ice and the egg will freeze.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:48.260
So it's quickly onto the father's feet and snuggled under a fold of skin.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.810
The mothers need to go to sea.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.730
Producing such a big egg has taken a lot out of them.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:03:59.980
They have to eat.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.270
eat now and won't be back until spring.

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:08.630
After a short hike, they reach a break in the ice,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.160
take a gulp of air, and enter the element

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.460
where they're most at home.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.280
As for the fathers, they get a lingering sunset.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.940
It's the last sunlight they'll see for more than two months.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:46.260
and the last touch of warmth now they have darkness savage winds nothing to eat and nothing to do but

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.090
take their turn at the edge of the huddle and shuffle along on their heels as they keep their

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:59.790
eggs off the ice there is no

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.430
but they may get some comfort from the moon and the stars and the dancing southern lights.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:15.300
Instinct must tell them that the sun will return and with it, their mates.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.350
Further north, other penguins have other concerns.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.520
These chin straps are trying to make an island out of an iceberg.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:48.200
Chin straps and other small penguins won't return to Antarctica until spring, their time to breed.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:07.770
Icy footholds are irrelevant to birds that fly.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:14.250
Storm petrels and fairy prions can simply rest on the unremitting southern winds.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.560
A wandering albatross rides the gale.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.160
There are a few battered islands, including South Georgia.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:41.660
The second largest penguins, the Kings, breed here.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.970
The chicks have already hatched, and they huddle together waiting for their parents to bring them some food.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:59.700
They're kept warm by thick downy coats, so unlike their eventual adult feathers,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.300
that the first explorers here thought they were a new species, the woolly penguin.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.040
Sometimes a king penguin parent will turn up with food, but these chicks generally do without.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.410
This young albatross high up on a cliff has been alone and unfed for three months now

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.730
while its parents travel the southern seas.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:45.010
Many miles south and many degrees colder, the Emperor Fathers are still in darkness.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:56.050
Then, as the southern hemisphere begins to tilt back towards the sun,

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.700
there's a hint of a new year in Antarctica.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.280
The sun seems almost shy in its reappearance, just skimming along the horizon.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:24.240
The temperature is still 30 below, but the sun has sent a signal and it's now that the eggs hatch.

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:29.220
The egg is now a bird, a hungry one.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.180
But the father has nothing to feed it.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.340
If the mother doesn't bring food in a few days,

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.650
the father, who is himself starving,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.240
will have to head for the sea and leave the chick to die.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.980
From somewhere out there, the mothers come to the rescue.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:00.340
skill.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:47.540
When they left for the sea, it was only a short walk away.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.430
Since then, the winter has extended the sea ice,

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:54.370
and the trek back can be as far as 40 miles.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.540
They've run a gauntlet of leopard seals and killer whales,

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.420
but the injured keep going with the same determination as everyone else.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:58.260
The mother calls for her mate.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:27.180
celebrates their survival, their reunion, and their chick.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:45.190
When the greeting ceremony is over, it's time to transfer the chick.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:51.040
But the father's been through hell with this baby and doesn't always find it easy to let go.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.330
Eventually, he does, and the chick eats at last.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.240
A meal of fish and squid caught a mile underwater and carried 40 miles over ice.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.840
Now the fathers will finally go and get to eat too.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.700
Not all the mothers have come back.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.600
A chick finds itself abandoned and then adopted.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.160
Not all the surviving mothers have been able to find their mates.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.790
In fact, demand is greater than supply,

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.930
and females will actually fight over orphans.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.870
But in the end, a female can't raise a foundling by herself.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:03.800
And almost all abandoned chicks are abandoned again.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:16.460
One parent is needed to keep the chick warm while the other goes for food.

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:22.120
For the next nine months, the successful fathers and mothers will do this in shifts,

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.850
making and remaking the long slog over the sea ice.

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:29.970
In the early spring, the other...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.080
ice is at its greatest extent. Sometimes there are pit stops, thanks to whales and

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.810
Waddell seals which use their teeth to keep breathing holes open. The seals can

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:55.260
wear down their teeth and end up toothless and starved. Under the ice the

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:59.980
temperature is below freezing, but the water stays liquid because it's salty.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:14.120
And while the top of the ice is bleak and barren, just a few feet down is a busy, living world.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:23.700
Shrimp-like krill, a staple food in the Antarctic, come here for their winter growth,

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.970
relatively safe from penguins and seals, at least until the ice melts.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:36.700
This is a world that humans have only recently been able to explore.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:40.410
And nearly every day, something new is discovered down here.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:50.710
In 2005, for instance, scientists showed that the abundance of krill depended largely on the amount of winter sea ice.

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:59.960
The extent of the ice is affected by climate change, something many scientists have...

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:11.700
come here specifically to study. One thing they've also established is that the winter

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:17.930
storms around Antarctica are getting more severe. The sea is becoming more treacherous for penguins

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:19.150
such as the chinstrap.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.680
On South Georgia, these King Penguin Chicks are on the verge of starving.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.200
Very occasionally, parents have turned up with food,

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.960
but mostly the chicks have been living off their fat.

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:51.180
As spring gets closer, the wandering albatrosses begin to make regular visits to their nests.

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:56.160
They can range over 700 miles a day gathering food for their chick,

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:58.330
now the size of a swan.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:13.970
When spring does come, it arrives on these outer islands first.

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:23.140
And with spring, the king penguins return.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.980
Thousands of animals will come ashore on these outlying islands.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:37.020
Antarctic Islands. King penguins are relatives of emperors, but prefer warmer climates.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:55.000
Among the arrivals are three-ton bull elephant seals. They've come to fight.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.380
Winners will have as many as 50 mates.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:04.960
Losers won't have any at all.

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.610
Waves of penguins arrive.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:21.580
Chinstraps.

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:24.490
Macaronis.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:29.740
Gentus.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:37.640
and they all head for high ground.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.500
The flamboyant macaronis are the noisiest.

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:56.980
They're all here for the same reason, to produce chicks.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:08.520
80,000 king penguin chicks have been surviving on their fat.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:14.510
Their parents have been fishing in waters as far north as Australia and South Africa.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:19.950
Now they're back on South Georgia to continue with the chicks' year-long upbringing.

00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:29.960
Raising a king penguin chick takes longer than a year.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.480
which means that while some of the adults are still being parents,

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.350
some, non-breeders, are molting.

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:49.160
Some are courting, performing the penguin Pasa Doble.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:00.480
Thank you.

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:37.210
It's easy to tell which ones are still being parents.

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:41.680
Not only are they not molting or dancing, they're doing a lot of fishing,

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:45.300
looking for lanternfish and squid miles to the north,

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:48.770
where the Pacific, Atlantic and Southern Oceans merge.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:54.380
In the opposite direction, on the coast of Antarctica,

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:58.490
the sea is slowly warming and the sea ice is breaking up.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:10.640
There's a lot of ice left though. The sun is low and the midday temperature is 10 below.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:19.640
At the emperor colony, spring has made life easier.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:23.460
The edge of the ice is nearer than it used to be,

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:26.430
and getting food for the chicks is that much quicker.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:29.960
On the other hand, the...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:37.260
chicks are growing fast and are a lot more demanding the parents are in a

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:42.200
constant relay race to keep young stomachs filled as the sea ice melts a

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:59.530
little more each day so each day the trip is shorter Arctic is a strange

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.870
upside down sort of place with nothing much on the surface

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:05.940
and an abundance below it.

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:13.150
Sometimes it's easy to forget that penguins are birds,

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:16.330
but they are, and they're flying birds too.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.640
It's just that they live on top of their sky.

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:25.910
They hunt fish and squid like swallows catching insects.

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:36.190
No other birds fly faster underwater.

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:40.730
Emperor penguins can hold their breath for 20 minutes.

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:45.430
They can dive beyond the reach of sunlight a mile down.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:51.920
They hunt in an ocean that at this time of the year abounds with food.

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:56.250
It begins here, under the crumbling ice, with krill.

00:21:57.670 --> 00:21:59.970
Their refuge is melting, and they aren't safe.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:11.330
anymore from penguins seals and whales the ice is also releasing a boost of

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:19.890
minerals the Sun shining through the ice sparks the growth of green algae which

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:26.120
feed the krill their ice home disappears suddenly the krill have to take their

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:29.950
important place in the wider world and seeking safety and numb

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:36.870
they gather in colossal swarms, greater than the mass, the total weight, of any other animal on Earth.

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:48.500
