00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:20.040
These are, on this monolith, are 300,000 Antarctic petrels.

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:37.000
Snow petrels are delicate birds, no bigger than small doves.

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:46.010
Yet they fly beyond the ice-bound coastline and over a 10,000-foot ice cap to high mountain peaks that poke through the ice.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:59.960
Like Adelis, the petrels need bare rock to nest, and to them, these peaks are oases.

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:08.400
In the middle of a vast ice desert, here are these birds and nothing else.

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:18.220
All the water here, of course, is solid, either rock-hard ice or dust-dry snow.

00:28:18.540 --> 00:28:23.370
But the petrels can still bathe in the snow and melt it in their mouths to drink.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:31.940
Thank you.

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:19.800
The petrels make their nests in cracks in the ledges and have to dig the snow out first.

00:29:20.590 --> 00:29:25.130
The most southerly nest ever found was 144 miles inland.

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:29.820
An expedition for food might be a 400-mile round trip.

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:33.580
Nests are made of their own plucked feathers.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:43.750
Hundreds of lifeless miles farther inland lies the South Pole.

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:48.380
Every year, its marker has to be moved a couple of feet

00:29:48.380 --> 00:29:51.320
because the mile-thick ice beneath it is moving,

00:29:51.770 --> 00:29:53.670
flowing towards the edge of the continent.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:56.730
It will take several thousand years,

00:29:57.160 --> 00:29:59.940
but the ice that's here now will eventually fall.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:16.820
to the sea. Throughout the past century, adventurers, explorers, and scientists have been drawn

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:23.030
to this point on the planet. Many lives have been lost and heroes proclaimed. And thanks

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:28.850
to their efforts, we have begun to understand a terrain that might as easily exist on Mars

