The parent who's just arrived will feed the chick,

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:46.810
while the one who hasn't eaten for a couple of days will go down and fill up with krill.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:51.520
Nests are in such precarious places to avoid predators,

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:57.280
something that's harder to do once they reach the water.

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:06.780
A leopard seal can eat penguins at a rate of six an hour.

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:09.900
In a whole summer, it can down thousands.

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:17.540
It's possible, if very rare, for a penguin to be caught and to get away,

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:25.880
despite the torments of skewers it soldiers on.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:36.180
A leopard seal, like any other predator, goes after the animals that are the easiest to catch.

00:36:36.980 --> 00:36:41.120
This penguin was probably already in poor condition before it was attacked.

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:49.890
In fact, all the animals on the islands around the Antarctic Peninsula are to some extent struggling.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:59.890
By 2005, most of the fish and 80% of the krill were gone, decimated by the huge Antarctic fishing fleet and

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.840
shrinking sea ice. This penguin has been particularly unlucky.

00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:11.340
But what's in the future for all the penguins?

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:22.980
Climate change, as elsewhere in the world, is having mixed effects.

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:27.460
The Antarctic Peninsula, which stretches up towards South America,

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:29.100
has warmed dramatically.

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:35.060
The assumption was that the same was happening on the mainland

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:38.870
and that aerial and satellite surveys would confirm that.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:42.550
Not so.

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:47.990
The surveys have shown that more snow than usual is falling on Antarctica

00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:51.000
and that the ice cap is actually thickening.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.950
Around the peninsula though, temperatures have risen five times faster than the world

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:05.510
average.

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:09.420
The ice is breaking up.

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:18.900
Ice shelves the size of small countries have floated away.

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:26.320
Antarctica is building up in the middle and crumbling at the edges.

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:40.550
It appears that the continent with virtually no pollution or people

00:38:40.550 --> 00:38:44.480
is the one affected by changes in the atmosphere the most.

00:38:45.940 --> 00:38:49.720
Scientists still need to know precisely what the effects are.

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:54.450
For help in that, they've looked more intently at what may already be

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:57.890
the best-studied seabird in the world, the Adélie penguin.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:02.820
A dailies live in a world of ice.

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:06.520
They feed under pack ice and rest on ice flows.

00:39:08.230 --> 00:39:10.550
Nowadays, though, the ice is unreliable.

00:39:15.910 --> 00:39:20.100
Dailies famously can put up with hardship, but these problems are new.

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:24.920
Where there used to be ice, now there's open water.

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:28.840
And the air is the warmest it's been since the last ice age.

00:39:31.370 --> 00:39:41.840
Chicks wait for their parents on what used to be frozen ground, but is now as muddy as a pigsty.

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:52.420
This colony used to be the most southerly outpost. It's not anymore.

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:56.520
Other Adelis are setting up colonies even farther south.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.610
Following the Adelis' redistribution, scientists can trace the effects of the changing pack ice.

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:09.740
The Adelis keep having new experiences.

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:11.750
Invaders, for instance.

00:40:13.400 --> 00:40:19.780
Chin-strap penguins, which avoid pack ice, do better in places where the Adelis are suddenly uncomfortable.

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:24.660
Fur and elephant seals are turning up, too.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:36.680
The Adelis can't move, not this year anyway, not before their chicks are grown, so they're stuck in the mud.

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:45.470
And as the long twilights turn into actual sunsets and short nights, the parents have to go farther away for pack ice and food.

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:57.620
When a parent comes back, its chicks recognize its call and are led away on a long steeplechase over the rocks to a place where they can be fed in peace.

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:05.450
Adelies usually have two chicks each, and the most aggressive one is always fed first.

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:08.960
As the pack ice shrinks, food becomes scarce.

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:13.830
Skewers are particularly alert to weak chicks that have not been fed.

00:41:20.680 --> 00:41:23.240
Sometimes strong chicks are attacked as well.

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:29.120
A frightened penguin often regurgitates its krill, and the skewers gratefully eat that too.

00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:47.280
Tension between skewers and penguins increases as summer nears its end.

00:41:47.960 --> 00:41:51.340
Both know it's important to fatten up before they leave for the winter.

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:59.970
Until a few years ago, this was solid ice. Now it's just...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:03.520
broken ice flows and the water is warmer and saltier.

00:42:17.670 --> 00:42:21.070
The emperor penguin colony is nearer than it's ever been

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:22.130
to the open sea.

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:29.180
The chicks this year are very healthy.

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:33.810
They're seven months old and already as big as adult Adelie's.

00:42:35.420 --> 00:42:40.710
Being right next to the sea means that their parents have quick access to fish and squid

00:42:40.710 --> 00:42:42.610
and can feed them more frequently.

00:42:43.960 --> 00:42:44.940
That's the good news.

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:49.370
The bad news is that the ice is still melting back

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:52.080
and the chicks don't have their swimming feathers yet.

00:42:53.690 --> 00:42:57.730
It's possible that the whole colony will disappear into the sea.

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:07.080
There's trouble at emperor colonies on several parts of the Antarctic coast.

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:08.960
Many are getting smaller.

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:12.400
Some, where they can, are moving farther south.

00:43:14.660 --> 00:43:18.450
It's not known if a lot of chicks have been drowning, but it's possible.

00:43:25.260 --> 00:43:29.970
On South Georgia, though, a thousand miles to the north, the King Pendant.

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:38.050
are flourishing. They breed on land and are used to warmer waters, so climate change has

00:43:38.050 --> 00:43:44.660
made little difference to them. A hundred years ago, they almost went extinct, hunted for skins

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:50.790
and meat. But now, as other penguins decline, they could march south into new territories.

00:43:51.650 --> 00:43:53.890
Kings are definitely on the up.

00:44:40.910 --> 00:44:43.190
A new generation goes to sea.

00:44:44.180 --> 00:44:47.020
In changing times, there are also winners.

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:55.290
The albatross chick is a year old and still growing,

00:44:55.710 --> 00:44:59.430
but it's old enough to start learning to do what albatrosses are famous for.

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:11.170
It spent its first winter on the nest, it will spend its second on the wing as a new generation is starting again.

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:16.730
For albatrosses, the end of summer is the beginning of the year.

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:24.860
The new chick will be fed throughout the autumn and will then be abandoned to spend winter alone.

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:29.540
This time next year, it too will finally graduate.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:40.980
almost perpetual flight. Early in autumn the sea ice is at its minimum and with

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:50.150
each year warmer than the last this is the minimum of the minimum. The emperor

00:45:50.150 --> 00:45:56.120
colony is gone, only some swimming birds are left. There's no way of knowing how

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:59.860
many of the chicks born nine months ago in midwinter actually survived.

00:46:04.190 --> 00:46:08.390
There's more to this story, though, than just warmer seas and melting ice.

00:46:14.130 --> 00:46:17.870
Because the effects of global warming are so evident in the Antarctic,

00:46:18.390 --> 00:46:21.590
the handful of scientists who were working here 30 years ago

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:24.100
have been joined by thousands of others.

00:46:25.770 --> 00:46:29.970
What they're finding is a lot less predictable than just a gentle warming.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.760
In fact, the increase in snow over the continental ice cap is making it colder.

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:43.410
Air at a temperature of 50 below spins out of the center in huge cyclones.

00:46:51.030 --> 00:46:55.070
Storm force winds and waves batter the fresh sea ice.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.000
as well as the animals that depend on it.

00:47:07.300 --> 00:47:09.320
The sea can be cold enough to freeze,

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:11.550
but the autumn storms won't let it.

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:17.930
In the Adelie colony on the edge of the continent,

00:47:18.590 --> 00:47:20.910
blizzards leave the parents hunkered down

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:22.230
or stranded offshore.

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:27.260
But penguins are hardy animals.

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:29.960
The real killer is what can happen next.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:37.910
The winds can drop, the water calms, and the super-cold continental air quickly freezes the sea.

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:47.170
The adults might make it across, but few of the chicks would survive the trek.

00:47:48.980 --> 00:47:53.500
Unpredictable storms break up and refreeze the sea many times each autumn.

00:47:54.450 --> 00:47:59.780
A daily chicks are growing waterproof feathers as fast as they can, as soon as they are able.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:02.020
they follow their parents down to the shore.

00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:09.480
The sea is full of heavy, jagged chunks of ice,

00:48:09.770 --> 00:48:12.910
and the youngsters are naturally wary of taking the risk.

00:48:13.210 --> 00:48:16.880
But what's their choice? Stay here and starve?

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:24.160
They can't get very far into the water, though,

00:48:24.550 --> 00:48:26.570
and end up skittering across the broken ice.

00:48:46.120 --> 00:48:51.560
All the clumsy thrashing and hopping brings up another danger, a leopard seal.

00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:09.830
It won't get every chick it tries for, but it knows a good hunting spot when it sees one.

00:49:13.550 --> 00:49:16.350
Finally, the fledglings take their first flight.

00:49:23.200 --> 00:49:27.600
Any youngsters that didn't take the plunge are quickly isolated and abandoned.

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:33.490
As the South Polar Cyclones get even colder, they'll freeze.

00:49:43.550 --> 00:49:48.550
As autumn advances, so does the sea ice at an average of a few miles a day,

00:49:48.900 --> 00:49:51.320
and penguins are driven north ahead of its edge.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:57.040
Chin straps, Adelis, and most of the other penguins

00:49:57.040 --> 00:49:59.200
will spend the winter at sea fishing.

00:50:04.090 --> 00:50:07.210
They cling to icebergs and generally wait for spring.

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:22.890
One kind is heading south.

00:50:24.290 --> 00:50:25.010
The Emperors.

00:50:34.190 --> 00:50:36.750
Some emperors are pioneering new colonies.

00:50:37.580 --> 00:50:40.270
They may be young, breeding for the first time.

00:50:40.910 --> 00:50:42.890
They may have lost their home to the sea.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:02.380
farther south so the ice won't melt under them.

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:06.500
It will be colder there in winter too.

00:51:12.670 --> 00:51:14.630
At the same time, under their feet,

00:51:15.020 --> 00:51:18.140
the fresh sea ice is the roof of an Antarctic nursery.

00:51:19.370 --> 00:51:22.410
In almost total darkness and safe from penguins,

00:51:22.940 --> 00:51:24.160
krill are growing again.

00:51:30.030 --> 00:51:38.140
The vital nurseries are changing with the climate and a few feet above them scientists are trying to work out what's changing and where.

00:51:42.530 --> 00:51:45.290
Climatologists see an Antarctic in turmoil.

00:51:48.270 --> 00:51:52.730
Biologists say many animals will lose, but some will gain.

00:51:54.640 --> 00:51:56.260
Politicians are talking about it.

00:51:57.300 --> 00:51:59.980
Some accept the science, others say it's unacceptable.

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:05.310
In fact, nobody really knows where we're heading.

00:52:14.980 --> 00:52:17.460
As for the penguins, they're tough animals.

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:23.880
They've coped with Antarctica up till now.

00:52:24.930 --> 00:52:26.750
Perhaps they can adapt to its future.

00:52:36.880 --> 00:52:40.520
Midwinter, and each emperor father has his egg.

00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:50.600
Each one is hungry, cold, and in the dark.

00:52:51.020 --> 00:52:59.500
In the most extreme place on Earth, the penguins of the Antarctic.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:00.700
Soldier on.
